    'What is open, and why it is not drawn. 1,587 candidate lines on 668 pages: 1,097 on 527 counted pages and 490 on 141 pages another session saved, which the table of counted pages does not hold. 485 of them are earlier passages of that session\'s pages as five further readers sorted them (the last item of the next list), and 50 are lines added after the census (70 were added in all: 31 by the builder after the twenty-second reading, two after the twenty-third, four from the reader of nine pages added on Nov 17, seven after the twenty-fourth, four after the twenty-fifth, seven after the twenty-sixth, six after the third round of readers of the other session\'s pages and nine after the twenty-eighth; nine of the 70 are on pages outside the table; eighteen belong to two acts that are now drawn on a tie line, and two to an act a chip carries). 180 of the 1,587 belong to twenty-three acts that a chip or a tie line words in part, 100 of them to one act, Megaspeed\'s purchases, of which the Times\'s first report gives only the start. They are the background of the affair as the coverage recounts it: earlier events of the relationship, Megaspeed\'s purchases and dealings, earlier approvals, remarks and lobbying, much of it relayed from the papers\' own reports, whose chips carry some of it. '
