# the third round of readers of the other session's pages (Nov 17; batch s9r of the rows), released with the earlier passages in the register
AFTER_ROUND3 = [
    "What was read. For 89 of the pages another session saved, the saved HTML holds the piece while the text file given to the first readers was a stub or a fragment. Three further readers of another company's model read them from the HTML against the acts as they stood before the second iteration of the take-in (375 dated acts), with two chips held out of each reader's table: five of the six came back as candidates, and the sixth test was faulty (a byline planted on a subscriber's stub). They returned 601 records on 58 of the pages, 262 of them candidates: 74 placed in the fifteen days, 21 with no period and 167 before Oct 29. Three more readers read the same pages for remarks, bylines and each page's own day; the builder has not yet read their results.",
    "What the builder did. It read on its saved page each of the 95 candidates of the days or with no period, in three parallel sessions of its own (one per reader), each against the actor's chips as they stand now, and read every row written from them again against its passages before writing it. `research/nv8_take_in_round3.tsv` gives each record one disposition: 39 were already carried, most by chips that the second iteration of the take-in had drawn from the same page; 19 are parts added to chips; 10 are relays of a response already drawn; 10 are requests that went unanswered, which the figure does not draw; 9 are earlier acts, for the register; 3 are the same act as another record; 3 are not acts; and 2 are new acts. By the builder's reading, of the thirteen left undrawn (the unanswered requests and the three that are not acts) twelve cut neither way and one for Huang or Nvidia. That is the builder reading its own figure: no independent reader has read these dispositions or rows.",
    'Anthropic and OpenAI, read first in every session. No record of the three readers names either company (a search of every cell, with a control). The builder\'s sessions also looked through the pages themselves, and one found, in a sidebar teaser, a report about Anthropic that no chip held. The aggregator\'s snapshots of Nov 10, which are saved, list it: Reuters reports that Anthropic "sent out and then retracted details of its blockbuster IPO filing, according to emails to bankers seen by Reuters late on Monday"; in the listing\'s summary the copy showed "plans to raise up to ~$69B", and the company "then followed up saying the pricing details were inaccurate". Anthropic\'s row already draws the filing, so the report is drawn on that row, on the day of the listing and with a dashed edge, since the listing gives the sending no day of its own. Only the listing is cited: the Reuters report itself is not saved, the builder\'s tools cannot fetch Reuters, and the page is on a list for the owner to save by hand. The act cuts against Anthropic, whose model builds this figure. Elsewhere on those pages the two names occur only in teasers and navigation that are dated after Nov 12 or are off the matter. A lead not yet followed: a press index lists transcripts, not saved, of television interviews of Nov 6 with OpenAI\'s finance chief and of Nov 11 with a cloud company\'s chief.',
    'Two more new acts. The head of Taiwan\'s immigration agency, before a committee meeting of the legislature on Nov 11 in Taipei, told reporters that agencies have records of the former executive\'s visits and "declined to say whether she would be placed on a blacklist": a refusal by an official about the government\'s own step, drawn on the local day its source names. And CNN reports on Nov 12 that there "are now questions brewing inside the White House over why the Commerce Department did not put Megaspeed on" the entity list; the report names no official and gives the questions no day, and it is drawn on its report\'s day as an act of its own, beside the chip that gives Reuters\'s report of that day about the Commerce secretary.',
    'Twenty-one chips amended. One row was wrong: the chip on Fortune\'s list of the company\'s earlier statements had customers ask Huang to visit, following a relay\'s bracket; the statement as first printed has the company\'s field teams ask, and the chip now says so, with three parts of the statement of December 2025 that Bloomberg recalls. One chip put a sentence after "The release says" which the release does not say; the page it cites says it, crediting the company. Answers that had been cut short are given whole: three senators\' and the Speaker\'s answers to Semafor on Nov 9 (one, quoted in a single sentence, was a caution, and its other two sentences now say so; another now comes with the question it answers), a senator\'s post of that day, and a company statement of Oct 29. The Times\'s first report gains what it says of Huang himself: that when the president was first briefed "U.S. officials did not believe the Nvidia executive knew" what she was doing, that "It is unclear whether intelligence officials have since updated their assessment", and that "some senior officials inside the Trump administration doubt that Mr. Huang could have remained unaware". The company\'s reply to that report gains the Times\'s sentences that it "has said that it complies with the law and that there is no evidence of its chips being smuggled to China" and that China\'s government has "blocked U.S. AI chips, protecting its own industry". Singapore\'s statement of Nov 12 is now given in its own words: the information was such as "she knew to be inaccurate" and was given "to downplay her role in Megaspeed", and "Other cases in the past have been dealt with in a similar way"; the chip had said only that it was inaccurate, and it now also gives the statement\'s dates for its requests to the United States. The chip on Commerce\'s remote-access rule gains the second step that the same report gives, the completion of the repeal of an earlier rule, which loosens where the first step tightens. The chip on Axios\'s report of Nov 11 gains, from a counted page, that the under secretary "had also received repeated warnings about Megaspeed in deputy-level meetings". The board\'s hiring of outside counsel is now cited from the statement itself; the first shareholder complaint is given with its class period and the several statements it attacks; the trade representative\'s remarks gain his hedge ("we remain concerned"); and four chips gain parts from pages of the day (two statements of the company, a House chairman\'s remark on further hearings, an undertaking of Taiwan\'s interior minister).',
    "A group on the figure's tie lines, and an industry council. Reuters's report of Nov 9 on the chip bills, saved in an automated French translation, sets side by side two voices the figure had not drawn: an industry council that counts Nvidia among its members and opposed the bills, whose spokeswoman refused to comment on Nvidia's situation; and the spokesman of Americans for Responsible Innovation, a Washington nonprofit in favour of the bills, to which Open Philanthropy has made awards that the tie lines give. He said that the obstacle for the bills is winning the White House's support, and that it would be costly for the White House to back Nvidia in a standoff with Congress. Both are now on the company's chip of Nov 9 that gives that report, in the French of the saved copy with an English gloss. The builder first wrote the group's statement as a tie line of its own and withdrew it before release: such a line must end at a drawn actor, and the group is not one. Whether its spokesman has ties that belong on the figure has not been looked up.",
    "The register, corrected. An act the builder registered in the last answer, that White House and intelligence officials had pressed for action against Megaspeed since the fall of 2025, named the wrong source: the builder credited the New York Times, whose saved report does not hold the sentence. Two saved pages credit Axios; the entry now says so and has their lines. That act is now marked as worded in part (the chip on Axios's report gives the resistance and the warnings, not how long the pressure had lasted), and so is the announcement of the repeal of the earlier rule. Three groups that recall the company's assertion that there is no evidence of smuggling are carried by its chip of Oct 29, which now gives the Times's sentence. Three more, which recall the company's own review of Megaspeed, are marked as worded in part by the chip that now gives its statement of December 2025; the builder's search of the open groups before the close found them. Notes on what a page adds were written on several groups. Of the nine records marked as earlier acts, five name one act of the register and are added to it as lines from pages outside the table; the other four name no single act (which of several open groups they belong to is not proved) and wait with the 167.",
    "Not done. The 167 candidates the three readers placed before Oct 29, and four of the nine above, are in no table yet. The three remarks readers' results are not read, so the count of pages still waits. Five long pages wait to be read in excerpts. From the sessions' notes, not yet acted on: further lines for five acts of the register; the company's assurances to Bloomberg of November and December 2025; four differing sums for Megaspeed's purchases; an earlier refusal; the order of a briefing and a trip of May 2025; parts for four chips; and the Journal's article on who introduced the two, which is saved and which no chip of the Journal words. Leads: the two television transcripts; the Reuters report on Anthropic's filing; a look-up of the nonprofit's spokesman.",
    "When this was released the figure carried 417 dated acts, 181 of them on their report's day. No independent reader has read this round. Two readings of the two releases before it landed while it was being closed (the next two items).",
    'The twenty-seventh reading landed while this was being closed. It read the second iteration of the take-in from a frozen copy and closed `audit: incomplete unfinished whole-source readings, disposition census, earlier-account comparisons and Part C sample`: it read every cited source for 52 of its 99 rows, finished 25 of 123 comparisons of dispositions, and did not draw its sample of pages. By its marks: three problems of a row\'s wording or provenance, one occasion on no chip and not in the register, one missing part, four faults in a table or the text, none in a gauge; no chip among the rows it finished needs to move to another day or row. On Anthropic and OpenAI it finds the placing of the prospectus chip coherent and the senators\' letter accurately stated; it asks that the answer set beside the report about OpenAI on an analyst\'s chip be named as Nvidia\'s; and it finds that a newsletter names the launch of Anthropic\'s roadshow, which the take-in\'s table calls no act ("no act/no day is too categorical"). It adds: "I cannot certify even-handed coverage across the two companies from this incomplete reading". The occasion it found on no chip is a senator\'s renewal, reported by Fortune, of her questions about a server maker; it cuts against Nvidia. When this was released nothing of the reading was answered; the answer is the next release.',
    'The twenty-eighth reading landed then too. It read the answer to the twenty-sixth reading from a frozen copy and closed `audit: incomplete full earlier-wording search and recorded source/eligibility gaps`. Of the answer\'s eleven rows it found seven right and four to qualify. By its marks: one row to move (the change on Megaspeed\'s website, drawn on Nov 11, is described by a counted newsletter of Nov 10), fifteen occasions on neither a chip nor the register, three missing parts, eleven faults in a table or the text, none in a gauge; 80 of the 94 answers hold and 14 hold in part. On Anthropic and OpenAI it finds that the answer "materially improves parity" and that the treatment is "still not established as even in coverage of comparable saved facts". It lists seven saved facts on no chip and in no register entry: Nvidia\'s specific commitment of $10 billion to Anthropic\'s stock; AMD systems deployed at Anthropic and at OpenAI; OpenAI\'s investment in a cloud company and its purchases from it; OpenAI\'s capital raise; OpenAI\'s development of its own chips; and $10 billion of new deals with Microsoft and Google that Anthropic reportedly disclosed. It would have the builder\'s exception reach three open groups: the Stargate partnership, an open-weight letter and an open-security alliance. It also found four register groups counted as open that a chip or a tie line words, and, in a sample of thirteen counted pages, one with an earlier act its census reader had not listed. Of the omissions it says that they "cut in more than one direction". When this was released nothing of the reading was answered; the answer is the next release, the lines on the two companies first.',
]
