AFTER_READING28 = [
    'What the reading read and found. It read the answer to the twenty-sixth reading (eleven rows, the register as changed, the 94 answers, the changed text and the map) from a frozen copy, and closed incomplete for a search of differently worded earlier accounts that it did not finish and for gaps of sources. Seven of the eleven rows are right and four need a qualification. By its marks: one row to move, fifteen occasions on neither a chip nor the register, three missing parts, eleven faults in a table or the text, none in a gauge. 80 of the 94 answers hold and 14 hold in part. It read four more whole statements against their chips and found no further part.',
    'Anthropic and OpenAI, answered first. The reading finds that the last answer "materially improves parity" and that the treatment of the two companies is "still not established as even in coverage of comparable saved facts". It lists seven saved facts on no row; all seven are drawn now, each read by the builder on its page. On the line between Anthropic and Nvidia, from CNBC\'s account of the amended prospectus: "the chipmaker has agreed to purchase another $10 billion worth of its Class N common stock immediately following the offering", in "a private placement that will not be registered in the offering"; the page does not say how that sum stands to the seven companies\' $26 billion. On the line between Anthropic and AMD and Broadcom, and on OpenAI\'s: "AMD\'s Helios is now deployed" at five companies, Anthropic and OpenAI among them. On a new line of OpenAI\'s: "OpenAI, another investee, also buys CoreWeave compute. To close the loop, OpenAI invested directly in CoreWeave"; "OpenAI has raised $15 billion in its latest funding round at a $1.25 trillion valuation", on the word of sources, for which a newsletter gives $11 billion; and "some of Nvidia\'s biggest customers, including Google and OpenAI, develop their own chips". On a new line of Anthropic\'s: "Anthropic discloses $10 billion in new deals with Microsoft and Google", from a newsletter that says neither what the deals are nor how the sum is split.',
    'The builder\'s exception, widened as the reading advised. The builder draws records about Anthropic and OpenAI when it finds them, without waiting for the owner\'s decision on earlier acts. The reading found three open groups the exception should reach, and they are on the tie lines now: Nvidia\'s part in the Stargate Project, whose page names neither company and is joined with a saved report that says "OpenAI announced the Stargate Project, a new venture backed by SoftBank, OpenAI, Oracle and MGX"; the open letter Huang wrote, "signed by 25 companies", which a page explains by the debate over the two companies\' appeals to Washington; and the alliance Nvidia joined in July, which Decrypt reports in its own voice beside an advocate\'s allegation about the two companies. The builder\'s search of the open groups afterwards found two more groups that those lines now word, and one they word in part. The owner has not ruled on the exception or on its width; the reading calls its two wider applications recommendations.',
    "One row moved. The chip on the wording of Megaspeed's website sat on Nov 11, the day of CNBC's report. A counted newsletter of Nov 10 already describes it, and a saved relay shows that Bloomberg reported the change in December 2025. The chip is on Nov 10 now, the day of the first saved account in the fifteen days, and says that the change is not one of these days.",
    'Parts added to two chips. The chip on Commerce\'s remote-access rule gains the duties the same account gives: the seven countries "will also be required to notify the Department of Commerce of the recipients and intended uses of the chips, and be subject to ongoing monitoring", and the exempt data centers "must report their local customers and computing scale". The chip on the company\'s lobbying gains two subjects: it "also disclosed lobbying on quantum computing and energy matters in the third quarter". The last answer said of that chip that the figure does not name lobbying firms; that was too wide, since three tie lines that record a lobbyist\'s former post with a senator name the firm, one for Anthropic and two for Nvidia. The sentence is corrected.',
    "The register. Four groups the reading found worded in part are marked so: the Times's article of October 2025, Singapore's questioning of June 2025, and Nvidia's investments in two cloud companies, which a tie line names without their sums. Nine earlier acts it found on counted pages are registered and not drawn: a write-off of about $4.5 billion in chips made for China, which CNBC sets after the company's statement about write-offs; a $6 billion license; investments in two start-ups, in Intel and in three other listed companies; and Musk's announcement that SpaceX would use only Nvidia's processors. The first stood on one of the thirteen counted pages of its sample, and its census reader had not listed it. The register now counts 950 groups, 112 carried and 838 open, twenty-three of the open ones worded in part. What the image carries of earlier acts is the owner's pending decision.",
    "Text and tables. Hunterbrook's article is dated Nov 10 in the table of pages, by its page data; the page itself prints Nov 6. The first tie line that cites it now says so, and that which stamp is right is not settled. Two census lines no longer say that Nvidia's investment in OpenAI is on no row. A remainder of the take-in's list gives both counts of pages. Seven recount lines on remarks differ between the two copies the reading compared, because the table of remarks is recomputed against the chips at every build; nothing was looked up again.",
    "The reading's own remainders, carried as limits: the search for differently worded earlier accounts of every part of the eleven rows; a complete resolution of the names of people, suppliers and financiers tied to the two companies; the members' counts and the look-ups, compared and not recounted; one page saved only as a subscription preview. Each open point has a written disposition (75 in all: 57 applied, 18 stated as a limit).",
    "When this was released the figure carried 417 dated acts, 181 of them on their report's day, and 69 tie lines between actors. No independent reader has read this answer. Not yet read: 64 folders that another session of this project saved while this answer was being closed. By their titles 32 are pages of 2025 and 2026 about the positions of Anthropic, Microsoft and Amazon on chip export controls, Anthropic's own statements among them, and one of the 32 is stamped Nov 12, 2026; by their names the other 32 are searches of lobbying disclosures on the chip bills. The builder reads them next, the pages about Anthropic first.",
]
